  "query_preview": "(Today through Monday)...\nIssued at 257 AM EST Tue Nov 3 2026\n\nA low pressure system and parent deep trough currently located near \nthe Central Plains will lift northeastward through the day today. \nThe low pressure system is expected to continue deepening as it \npushes into the Midwest before eventually occluding on Wednesday. \nMeanwhile, a strong LLJ continues to shift eastward which will help \nto advect warm, rich Gulf moisture northward late today into \ntonight. \n\nIncreasing dynamics from the approaching system combined with deeper \nmoisture surging northward supports widespread precipitation by late \ntonight. The greatest precipitation chances are from late tonight \nthrough Wednesday morning as a narrow corridor of strong isentropic \nascent moves across an anomalously moist warm sector. The strongest \nforcing should then begin to shift east of the area Wednesday \nafternoon leading to lower coverage of showers. Lingering moisture\nand marginal forcing will likely still support scattered light \nshowers or drizzle at times though. QPF amounts over an inch \nappear possible across southeast portions of central Indiana, \nwhere the strongest forcing and deeper moisture are likely to \noverlap.\n\nDiurnal mixing into the strong LLJ may promote strong wind gusts up \nto 35 mph during the day today. Isolated higher gusts cannot be \ncompletely ruled out. Near record temperatures in the mid 70s are \nalso possible from the strong southerly flow. The forecasted high \ntemperature for Indianapolis this afternoon is 76F and the record \nhigh temperature is 77F. Clouds may limit overall heating slightly \nwhich leads to some uncertainty. It is worth noting though that the \nlatest guidance has trended towards less cloud cover during the day \nthanks to drier air briefly filtering in ahead of the low pressure \nsystem.\n  \nQuiet weather conditions are expected Wednesday night into Thursday \nonce drier air filters in behind a cold front. Model guidance \ngenerally depicts multiple shortwaves moving through the region \nwithin northwest flow aloft late this week through early next week. \nWeak forcing from these features could support low rain chances at \ntimes, mainly Friday and Sunday, but limited moisture return due to \nthe Gulf remaining cutoff leads to lower confidence. Cold air \nadvection will lead to cooler more seasonable conditions later this \nweek with highs in the upper 50s to low 60s by Friday. Seasonable \nconditions then persist through early next week, featuring highs \ngenerally in the mid to upper 50s and lows in the 30s to low 40s.",
